"""Original authored Language Atlas chapter outlines and bounded explanations.

Bibliographic codes link to public sources, not archived full source documents.
"""
CHAPTERS=[]
def add(title,area,definition,principle,reconstruction,limitation,sources):
    CHAPTERS.append({'title':title,'area':area,'definition':definition,'principle':principle,'reconstruction':reconstruction,'limitation':limitation,'sources':sources})

add('What a human language is and what a language model is not','Foundations',
'''Human language is a community-shared, learned system for expressing meanings, coordinating action, maintaining relationships, and transmitting culture. Linguists study its sound or sign form, grammatical patterns, semantics, use in interaction, acquisition, change, and social variation. A language is not reducible to a spelling system, a dictionary or a statistical model trained on texts. Spoken and signed languages organize linguistic form through different physical modalities yet both possess systematic combinations of meaningful units. The existence of a rule is an empirical claim about users and contexts, not a licensing decision by a machine parser. A reference to the language of a person or community requires self-identification, appropriate historical perspective, and evidence from its users.''',
'''Distinguish the representation levels: acoustic/visual gesture, phonetic or sign units, phonological contrasts, morphemes, phrases, semantic interpretations, discourse moves, and the social situation. Mapping between them is many-to-many rather than an invertible chain. Different messages can express the same proposition with different pragmatic force; the same sentence can be interpreted differently across situations. A language model estimates properties of a supplied distribution of strings or other representations. Statistical predictability does not imply the speaker's intended meaning, truthful content, human-like understanding or full sociolinguistic competence.''',
'''Choose one simple message such as “Could you open the window?” Identify its literal interrogative form, possible request function, relevant participants, context and possible translations. Preserve alternative interpretations rather than assigning a single universal speech act. Compare a corpus token count with the underlying social interaction and name which levels the count excludes.''',
'''Do not infer mental states or linguistic identity from a short message. Avoid treating an AI system's confidence about meaning as an independently checked interpretation. The archive provides educational models and selected source pages, not a universal theory of language.''',['SLP3','SEP-PRAG','UD-GUIDE'])

add('Speech articulation and the production of linguistic sound','Phonetics',
'''Speech uses airflow, voicing, and articulatory gestures by the respiratory system, larynx, tongue, jaw, palate and lips. Consonants can be classified by place and manner of articulation and voicing; vowels are described by tongue position, openness, lip rounding and other features. These labels are abstractions over continuous coarticulation, not fixed physical coordinates. Speech sound production also depends on individual anatomy, dialect, phonological context, health, and speaking style. Written spellings are not reliable phonetic recordings: a letter may stand for different phones or none, while one phone can have multiple orthographic representations.''',
'''A useful abstract phonetic token can be represented as a vector of independently declared features such as [voice], place and manner. A Hamming distance between two such vectors counts stipulated categorical differences, not physical acoustic dissimilarity or perceptual confusion. Some phonetic differences are continuous (formant trajectories, duration, fundamental frequency), and others involve timing and overlapping gestures. For a real measurement, record the microphone, sampling rate, environment and annotator; an IPA symbol is a transcription decision, not an instrument reading.''',
'''Compare toy bilabial stops /p/ and /b/ with the same place and manner but differing voice labels. Run the feature-distance reference on this supplied inventory. Then draw a vocal-tract diagram from a legitimate source and explain what extra data would be needed to measure actual voicing onset. Do not invent audio or a language's entire inventory.''',
'''IPA charts are an organized notation, not a claim that all languages use each symbol. No physiological or acoustic recordings were collected. Physical measurements require separate rights, instrument calibration and validation.''',['IPA-CHART','PHOIBLE','SLP3'])

add('Acoustic phonetics, speech signals and uncertainty','Phonetics',
'''Acoustic speech consists of time-varying pressure changes, usually measured by a transducer and digitized. Relevant analyses may include time-domain waveforms, spectrograms, spectral envelope, formants, pitch-related periodicity, duration, loudness and noise. No single number fully describes a spoken vowel or consonant. A physical fundamental-frequency estimate depends on the presence of periodic voicing and on the estimator and window; a child, an adult and a speaker with irregular phonation may produce very different signals. Coarticulation and environmental noise limit simplistic phone segmentation.''',
'''Sampling converts a continuous signal to a discrete series x[n] at sample rate f_s. For a truly band-limited input and ideal sampling, the Nyquist criterion requires f_s greater than twice the highest signal frequency for unaliased reconstruction. This does not imply speech itself is perfectly band-limited or that a poor microphone records every relevant articulation. The discrete Fourier transform summarizes a finite window's frequency content; time-frequency resolution trades off with window length. A spectrogram's apparent patterns must be tied to a disclosed window, normalization and color scale.''',
'''Construct a sampled sinusoid mathematically rather than using a real speaker's recording. Calculate sample period 1/f_s and compare hypothetical 500 Hz and 3 kHz components at 8 kHz sampling. Record why this does not characterize intelligibility. Describe how you would collect consented speech and test whether a measured F0 corresponds to a reported pitch.''',
'''No real audio data, speech-recognition benchmark or recording rights were acquired. Do not infer a person's identity, health or emotion from a toy spectrum. Acoustic measurements require calibration and independently checked labels.''',['SLP3','PRAAT','IPA-CHART'])

add('Signed languages, gesture, and visual linguistic form','Modalities',
'''Signed languages are naturally occurring languages expressed through manual and nonmanual articulators. Handshape, location, movement, orientation, facial expression and bodily timing can contribute to linguistic contrasts and grammatical structure. Signed languages are not merely word-by-word renderings of the surrounding spoken language. Their grammars, discourse and sociolinguistic histories deserve primary attention, including Deaf communities' expertise and leadership. Gesture and conventional sign are not identical categories, and a mixed multimodal interaction may include both. A linguistic archive that collects only orthographic text has failed to preserve much of the communicative system.''',
'''A visual utterance can be represented as multiple time-aligned tracks. A symbolic gloss may help analysts communicate, but it loses kinematic and interactional detail. A video annotation requires time intervals, informed permission and a separation between observable gestures, analyst-inferred linguistic features and community translations. Nonmanual signals can overlap manual signs, so a forced one-token-at-a-time linear text model is not a complete representation. Meaning is constructed through syntax, discourse, context and agreed conventions, not a universal hand-gesture dictionary.''',
'''Design an empty annotation schema with fields for manual track, facial expression, reference location, timestamp, linguistic judgment, researcher and consent status. Explain which gestures could overlap without declaring any real sign-language facts. Use community-reviewed teaching material before attempting a transcription example or cross-language correspondence.''',
'''This chapter contains no licensed sign-video corpus and claims no sign-language translation system. Consent, community governance and cultural context are central evidence requirements, not optional metadata.''',['LSA-SIGN','ELAR','UD-GUIDE'])

add('Phonemes, phones, and allophonic distribution','Phonology',
'''A phone is an analytically recognized speech sound; a phoneme is a unit of contrast within a particular language or variety. A minimal pair can support phonemic status when two forms differ in a targeted sound and differ in meaning, but uncontrolled spelling, tone, stress or morphological distinctions can confound the inference. Allophones are contextually distributed realizations of a phonemic category under a particular analysis. Contrastive and complementary distributions are useful evidence, not infallible algorithms that decide linguistic structure from a small word list.''',
'''A feature matrix abstracts possible oppositions. In a constructed system, /p/ and /b/ may differ by one voicing dimension, while [n] and [ŋ] may be linked by an assimilation rule before a velar consonant. Both analyses need corpus support and speaker judgment before being claimed for a real language. A phonological rule is a mapping conditioned by environment and grammar; a phonetic articulatory tendency can motivate it without making the rule universal. Rule ordering and representation choices can yield different apparent analyses.''',
'''Use the explicit feature-vector tool to compare two constructed phonemes. Create three invented lexical forms, change only one segment, and say which additional semantic and speaker evidence would make them a genuine minimal pair. Then construct a rule that changes a nasal place label before a velar stop, and identify its assumed domain.''',
'''Counting features is not a validated auditory distance or phoneme inventory discovery. Published PHOIBLE inventories use different analytical decisions; source dataset/version must travel with any comparisons.''',['IPA-CHART','PHOIBLE','WALS'])

add('Prosody: stress, tone, timing, and intonation','Phonology',
'''Speech communication is structured above the segment level. Stress can organize prominence in a word or phrase; lexical tone can distinguish words or grammatical categories; intonation patterns can help organize information, stance or discourse; duration and rhythm can carry contrasts. The boundaries between stress, tone, accent and intonation are language-specific and contested in individual analyses. A rising F0 contour does not universally signal a question, nor does falling F0 prove certainty. The same prosodic gesture can depend on voice quality, speaking rate and social convention.''',
'''A time-aligned prosodic annotation should distinguish observed physical contours from phonological categories and inferred social meaning. Relative timing, syllable boundaries and normalized pitch may be more interpretable than raw F0 in hertz across speakers. In a tonal language, tone-bearing units and morphological context must be specified. Information theory can quantify distributions of contours only after a stable measurement and label protocol; the numerical entropy itself cannot identify pragmatic meaning.''',
'''Draw three entirely hypothetical F0 trajectories over 300 ms and label them as mathematical contours, not real speech. State how a speaker-specific baseline and temporal alignment would change comparison. Then compare two linguistic analyses of the same rising contour under different contexts, preserving both as hypotheses.''',
'''No real F0 measurement, tonal-language judgment or sociolinguistic inference is made. A single pronunciation recording is insufficient to establish a community grammar or universal intonation semantics.''',['IPA-CHART','PHOIBLE','SLP3'])

add('Phonotactics, syllables, and sound-pattern restrictions','Phonology',
'''Phonotactics concerns restrictions and preferences on combinations of sounds in a language. Syllables organize segments into structures that may be described with onsets, nuclei and codas; phonological weight, sonority and stress can interact with syllable parsing. A sequence impossible in one stipulated toy grammar can be common in another language, so rejection by a parser does not mean the sequence is inherently unpronounceable. Loanwords, rapid speech, morphological boundaries and dialect variation complicate simple restrictions. Phonotactic generalizations should be supported by representative forms and speakers, not only by a restricted list of spellings.''',
'''A finite-state acceptor can encode permitted syllable shapes (for example a stipulated CV or CVC system). It can test membership efficiently but only relative to its declared alphabet and transition rules. Productive phonological patterning may be gradient, stochastic, feature-based or conditioned by morphological structure. A finite acceptor is not a theory of every speaker's competence; the model's outputs should be interpreted as “licensed by this grammar” and “not licensed by this grammar.”''',
'''Construct a toy inventory C={p,t}, V={a,i} and a CV grammar. Enumerate pa, ti and apt. Show which are generated and explain why this says nothing about real-language words. Extend the model to permit a final consonant and observe the changed accepted set.''',
'''Do not turn invented phonotactics into claims about specific minority languages. Real data require corpus provenance and community review, and syllable-boundary analyses may be disputed.''',['PHOIBLE','WALS','SLP3'])

add('Morphology: words, lexemes, morphemes and segmentation','Morphology',
'''Morphology studies how word forms relate to lexical meanings and grammatical functions. A lexeme is an abstract lexical unit, while a word form is a particular realization; a morpheme is a theoretical minimal meaningful unit whose surface realization may be conditioned by context. Segmentation can be ambiguous: an apparently meaningful substring may be coincidental, and a lexical item may not admit a clean concatenative analysis. Analysts must distinguish inflectional features, derivational relationships, clitics and compounds and must not assume that the English space-separated “word” is a universal linguistic unit.''',
'''A finite lexicon can map a surface string to multiple analyses with lemma, features and evidence provenance. It may return “unknown” when a form is missing, but that result means the lexicon is incomplete, not that the word is impossible. Finite-state transducers can encode regular relations between underlying and surface strings; this does not guarantee that every language's morphology is regular or concatenative. Lexicon source IDs and language variety must remain attached to the output.''',
'''Compare the invented surface form “flies” with analyses as a plural noun and present-tense verb. Use the morphology lookup to preserve both instead of choosing one without context. Add a constructed suppletive pattern, and explain why simply splitting the suffix cannot recover all inflectional structure.''',
'''The supplied entries are toy descriptions, not a universal morphological dictionary. An empty analysis list is an explicit coverage gap and must not be treated as a negative human-language judgment.''',['LEIPZIG','UD-GUIDE','SLP3'])

add('Inflection, derivation, and nonconcatenative morphology','Morphology',
'''Inflection often expresses grammatical categories associated with a lexeme, while derivation can create a related lexical entry or change category. The distinction is not mechanically obvious in every language: productivity, semantic transparency, category change and syntactic distribution can differ. Processes may include affixation, reduplication, internal vowel alternation, tone change, templatic patterns or suppletion. A model that assumes every word is stem+suffix is a narrow hypothesis. Good documentation records the full paradigm, its source, attested variation and any speaker-reported irregularities.''',
'''A paradigm is a relation between grammatical feature bundles and forms. Its cells can be empty, syncretic (same form expressing multiple feature bundles) or suppletive. A finite mapping supports multiple analyses and generation candidates, but a reverse mapping must preserve ambiguous cells rather than invent unique lemmas. Distinguish features that a source actually annotated from those inferred by the algorithm. A grammatical feature label is meaningful only under an identified annotation scheme and language description.''',
'''Build a small toy paradigm for a noun with singular/plural forms and a verb with two person features. Reverse the mapping to produce multiple analyses of a shared surface. Add one unavailable cell and mark it unknown; do not fill it by analogy without declaring a proposed hypothesis.''',
'''Rules learned from a handful of forms may overgeneralize. The Leipzig rules standardize interlinear gloss presentation but do not prove the correctness of an individual morphological analysis.''',['LEIPZIG','UD-GUIDE','WALS'])

add('Grammatical categories and cross-linguistic typology','Morphology',
'''Languages can grammatically encode tense, aspect, mood, case, agreement, number, gender or noun classes, evidentiality, definiteness, person and other features. No fixed menu of categories must appear in every language. A label that resembles an English category can behave differently elsewhere. Typology compares structural properties across languages while recognizing genealogical relatedness, areal diffusion, sampling unevenness and researcher decisions. The existence of a category is an empirical description grounded in paradigms and syntactic environments, not an assumption from a universal table.''',
'''A feature coding should include the language/variety, grammatical domain, attestation source, value definition, uncertainty, and sampling process. WALS features classify structural properties at a scale useful for comparative work, but values do not automatically express all exceptions or dialects. A cross-language statistical pattern may reflect inheritance, contact or selection bias rather than a universal constraint. Aggregating one value per language is not valid without choosing comparable observations and handling missing data.''',
'''Define a synthetic feature matrix for four invented languages and two categorical properties, with one unknown cell. Compare complete-case frequency against a misleading frequency obtained by coding unknown as absent. Explain what genealogical and regional controls a real typological study would require.''',
'''Do not apply a language-family label as a proxy for its speakers' identity or abilities. Recheck source version and avoid treating WALS as a complete census or independent original recording of every language.''',['WALS','UD-GUIDE','GLOTTOLOG'])

add('Lexemes, polysemy and lexical semantic networks','Semantics',
'''A lexeme may have several related senses, and a single written form can represent unrelated lexical entries. Synonymy is generally context-sensitive, while antonymy, hyponymy, meronymy and other relations require explicit criteria. A dictionary entry is an editorial representation of usage, not a complete enumeration of everything speakers can mean. Word sense disambiguation depends on syntactic environment, discourse, shared knowledge and world context. Frequencies and embeddings can suggest associations but do not independently prove precise meaning or correct translation.''',
'''Represent a semantic lexicon as typed relations between versioned sense IDs, not as an undifferentiated web of word strings. `bank` in a financial context and in a river context illustrates why word identity is not sense identity. A model should preserve unknown senses and evidence rather than treating a guessed gloss as certain. Even a correct sense relation does not prove that using it in a particular utterance matches speaker intent. Language change and social registers shift the applicability of entries.''',
'''Create a tiny fictional lexicon with two senses for one orthographic form. Add source contexts that favor each sense. Attempt to remove the contexts and observe that the word form alone cannot determine the sense. Represent one source's disagreement as a qualified relation, not a deletion.''',
'''Lexical databases have their own rights, coverage and editorial assumptions. This edition does not reproduce copyrighted dictionaries or claim a comprehensive lexicon of any language.''',['SLP3','SEP-COMPOS','WALS'])

add('Word boundaries are analytical choices, not whitespace','Writing and encoding',
'''Written spaces delimit some orthographic words, but the boundaries of meaningful or grammatical words can differ across scripts, clitics, compounds and languages. In many writing systems, words are not indicated by spaces; in others, spacing conventions differ across genres or historical periods. Computational tokenization must distinguish bytes, Unicode code points, grapheme clusters, word-like segments, morphemes and model tokens. Each choice produces a different count and may affect downstream evaluation. The mere output of a particular tokenizer is not evidence that it found natural language word boundaries.''',
'''Segmentation can be modeled as a constrained path problem over a finite lexicon. For a sequence of n code points, each path divides the string into dictionary entries. An additive weight can rank paths, but the lexicon and weights are supplied and may be incomplete. The bounded dynamic program in this package lists multiple segmentations with a cap. Truncation must be reported because an incomplete list does not prove the exact ambiguity count. Unicode default word-break algorithms are more complex and are versioned separately from any toy lexicon.''',
'''Use the invented string “therapist” and a lexicon containing “therapist”, “the” and “rapist”. List both parses without implying either is the intended reading. Then use an unknown word and record that it has no path in this lexicon—not that it is an impossible human word.''',
'''A finite dictionary method is not UAX 29, a full morphological parser, or a multilingual tokenization benchmark. Its outputs cannot ground real speech or linguistic correctness without source evidence.''',['UNICODE-UAX29','SLP3','UD-GUIDE'])

add('Writing systems, orthography, scripts and transliteration','Writing and encoding',
'''Writing uses conventional visible symbols to encode aspects of language, but the relationship between writing and speech or sign is variable. Alphabetic, abjad, abugida, syllabic, morphosyllabic and mixed systems involve different mappings. A script is not a language, and a language can use multiple scripts; multiple languages can share a script. Romanization or transliteration maps written forms under a chosen convention, whereas phonetic transcription aims to represent sound. Both can be lossy, and some transliterations are designed to be reversible only under special diacritics or escape conventions. A survivor needs source scripts, encoding, normalization rules, reading order and rendering information.''',
'''A reversible transliteration is an injective mapping over the declared input domain, with an inverse. When distinct source symbols map to one target string, the transformation is lossy unless additional side information is stored. Canonical Unicode equivalence may preserve linguistic identity for some purposes but compatibility normalization can merge distinct symbols. A digital archive must retain raw source bytes and a described transformation pipeline rather than saving only a transliteration. Rights and provenance also apply to community-maintained orthographies.''',
'''Design a toy alphabet mapping where two distinct symbols both become “a”; demonstrate why the original cannot be recovered from the output alone. Then add explicit escape codes to make the mapping injective, and document that the new code is an artificial demonstration, not a real writing system.''',
'''Do not assume Latin letters, left-to-right orientation or standardized spelling. Normalization and transliteration are different operations and require separate evidence and tests.''',['UNICODE-UAX15','BCP47','W3C-LANG'])

add('Unicode scalar values, UTF-8, and exact source bytes','Writing and encoding',
'''Digital text is stored as bytes under an encoding, often UTF-8. The Unicode standard assigns code points to abstract characters, but a displayed user-perceived character can consist of several code points. Code units, code points, grapheme clusters, words and model tokens must not be conflated. A raw file's exact bytes carry evidence for the original spelling, language tag, punctuation and normalization. Decoding malformed UTF-8 using replacement characters can irreversibly conceal corruption; this archive defaults to strict rejection. A rendering environment can also lack fonts even when the code points have been preserved correctly.''',
'''UTF-8 encodes Unicode scalar values as variable-length byte sequences. A round trip under strict encoding/decoding restores the original valid sequence, but not necessarily the appearance of an unavailable font. A code-point length is not a grapheme count: a base letter plus combining mark may represent one perceived character. A source digest identifies bytes relative to a trusted expected hash; it does not verify who authored or measured the text. Record the Unicode version used for any normalization operations.''',
'''Compare “café” written with a precomposed `é` and with `e` plus a combining acute mark. Count UTF-8 bytes and code points separately. Decode an intentionally malformed byte sequence with strict error handling and preserve the error rather than silently replacing it.''',
'''The Python runtime's Unicode database may lag behind the latest published Unicode version. Do not claim conformance to a later version simply because an official annex is current.''',['UNICODE-UAX15','UNICODE-UAX29','UNICODE-CORE'])

add('Canonical and compatibility normalization are different','Writing and encoding',
'''Unicode normalization addresses multiple encoded representations that are equivalent under defined rules. NFC and NFD concern canonical equivalence; NFKC and NFKD also apply compatibility transformations that can collapse visually or historically distinct characters. A program that normalizes all text with NFKC can change distinctions that matter for textual scholarship, mathematical notation or identifiers. The appropriate form depends on a declared use case. The source text should remain available for forensic and linguistic reconstruction even when a normalized search copy is created.''',
'''For a canonical-equivalent sequence x,y, Unicode normalization NFC(x)=NFC(y) under the same conformant Unicode version. This does not imply a reversible mapping from normalized output to original bytes. If a compatibility character becomes a different sequence under NFKC, retain both original and normalized forms. The package's Unicode profile reports raw byte SHA, code-point count, NFC/NFKC outputs and a compatibility-change warning; it deliberately does not compute user-perceived grapheme boundaries.''',
'''Normalize a decomposed accent and demonstrate equal NFC output. Normalize a circled digit and observe NFKC replacement with a plain digit. Keep the original raw form in a local notebook and explain why a historical document shouldn't be overwritten by a search index representation.''',
'''A normalization check is not a language identification method or full security policy for confusable identifiers. Record the Unicode database version and never silently call NFKC lossless.''',['UNICODE-UAX15','UNICODE-CORE'])

add('Grapheme clusters and segmentation across scripts','Writing and encoding',
'''Users perceive text in units that do not always match code points or visible glyphs. The Unicode Text Segmentation annex describes default grapheme, word and sentence boundary rules, with tailoring possible for language-specific behavior. Emoji sequences, joiner behavior, combining marks and script-specific clusters make naive one-codepoint counting unreliable. A display glyph may depend on font shaping and context, while the grapheme cluster is an algorithmic text-boundary concept. A program that counts code points correctly has not automatically implemented UAX 29's rules.''',
'''Boundary determination is a versioned procedure over Unicode properties and explicit rule precedence. A proper implementation must use the matching Unicode data tables and pass conformance test files. Simple regular expressions like `\\w+` or splitting at spaces do not implement the full annex. For preservation, retain source bytes, Unicode normalization version and any segmentation profile/tailoring. The package intentionally returns `grapheme_cluster_count = null` until a conformant grapheme implementation and its data tables are installed and tested.''',
'''Inspect a combining-accent string and an emoji ZWJ sequence, counting code points while leaving the perceived grapheme count unresolved. Use a fully licensed Unicode test suite in future work before asserting UAX conformance. Write down how a chosen script requires tailoring.''',
'''No full Unicode UAX 29 grapheme, word or sentence segmenter is implemented in this release; no Unicode 18.0 conformance test has been executed.''',['UNICODE-UAX29','UNICODE-UAX15','W3C-LANG'])

add('Interlinear glossing, transcripts and scientific accountability','Documentation',
'''An interlinear glossed text commonly aligns a source-language line with a morpheme-level analysis, a line of grammatical labels and a translation. The Leipzig Glossing Rules provide conventions for describing morpheme boundaries, clitics, grammatical categories and other annotation choices. Glossing is an *analysis*, not the primary sound/video observation; a different linguist may parse a form differently, and community members may reject an externally imposed category. An archive should retain source form, annotation tiers, analytic status, original orthography, recording metadata where relevant and the identity and consent status of contributors.''',
'''Alignment is a relation among tiers, not necessarily a one-to-one mapping among code points. One morph may have several grammatical functions and several forms may realize one category. Hyphens, equals signs, periods, and underscores can have stipulated glossing roles, so a converter must not strip or normalize them without declaring the transformation. If a gloss line contains more segments than the source analysis, record a mismatch or the permitted rule rather than silently shifting tokens. Separately preserve observed spelling, linguist judgment and free translation.''',
'''Create a fictional two-morpheme form and an aligned pair of gloss labels such as PL and NOUN, explicitly marking the example synthetic. Write a second analysis with an alternative boundary and explain what corpus, speaker, or paradigm evidence would discriminate them. Keep the original form in both analyses.''',
'''Leipzig conventions standardize presentation, not truth. No real language documentation from an endangered-language speaker or community is included; archival rights remain a separate requirement.''',['LEIPZIG','ELAR','UD-CONLLU'])

add('Formal grammars, constituency and derivation','Syntax',
'''A formal grammar specifies a set of expressions and derivational rules. A context-free grammar (CFG) can express nested constituency patterns, but no single CFG provided with this package is a grammar of every human language. Linguistic structure is established by evidence from distribution, interpretation, morphosyntax and speaker judgments. Phrase structure trees are useful hypotheses about constituent groupings, and multiple derivations of the same token sequence reflect structural ambiguity under a grammar. A parser returning a tree demonstrates only membership and a witness relative to its rules.''',
'''The Chomsky normal form subset used by CYK has lexical rules A→terminal and binary rules A→BC. For an input of n tokens, dynamic programming considers spans [i,j), split points k and rule categories. A state can store both a count of derivations and a sample witness. Counts can grow rapidly; if a cap is applied, exact ambiguity claims must stop. Epsilon productions, unary nonterminals and unknown words are not in this bounded implementation, even though broader grammars can represent them.''',
'''Use the supplied constructed grammar for “robots see astronomers with telescopes.” Enumerate the attachment of “with telescopes” to the noun phrase and verb phrase, and inspect two derivations. Compare with a different order that the toy grammar rejects, emphasizing that the grammar—not nature—determines this result.''',
'''No syntactic parse here verifies real acceptability, discourse interpretation or the intentions of a speaker. Many languages have structures that require grammar extensions, statistical modeling or competing theories.''',['CHOMSKY1956','SLP3','SEP-COMPOS'])

add('Dependency syntax and Universal Dependencies','Syntax',
'''Dependency grammar describes syntactic structure through relations between words or other nodes. In a basic dependency tree, most nodes have one head, a designated root has head zero, and cycles are forbidden. Universal Dependencies offers a cross-linguistic annotation framework with part-of-speech tags, morphological features and typed syntactic relations. A valid graph shape is not evidence that its labels are correct: a parser can create a formally well-formed tree that is linguistically implausible. Furthermore, word segmentation and empty elements can complicate a naïve one-row-per-word model.''',
'''CoNLL-U represents annotations in ten tab-separated fields per word line, with comments and blank sentence boundaries. It supports multiword token ranges, empty nodes and enhanced dependencies under special rules. The package implements only a declared **basic integer-word subset** with sequential IDs, exactly one root and acyclic HEAD links. It refuses multiword and enhanced structures rather than pretending complete CoNLL-U coverage. A tree's structural validity and source provenance should be audited independently from label accuracy and typological applicability.''',
'''Validate the constructed “Ada reads.” sentence with three rows. Change the last dependency head to itself to create a structural error; the checker must reject it. Then describe a real case with multiword tokens and explain why this restricted parser must abstain until a compliant extension is written.''',
'''The validator checks tree shape but not all formatting constraints or UD language-specific conventions. UD release versions and treebank licenses vary and should be pinned before importing source data.''',['UD-GUIDE','UD-CONLLU','SLP3'])

add('Word order, head direction and cross-language diversity','Syntax',
'''Language typology often describes the relative order of subject, verb and object in certain declarative clauses, but such categories simplify variation due to information structure, case marking, verb classes or dialects. “SOV” and “SVO” are descriptive labels for selected constructions, not immutable identities of entire communities. Nominal phrases, adpositions, adjective placement and other word-order features can correlate without forming inviolable universals. Language contact, shared ancestry and sampling designs affect claims about cross-language frequencies. The WALS project documents many structural features but is not a random sample of every recorded variety.''',
'''A comparative matrix should link each value to a defined construction, the source chapter, language variety, and confidence/uncertainty; it must distinguish missing evidence from a negative instance. Counting families or areas separately can help identify dependence, but a simple proportion across languages does not establish a causal mechanism. Structural changes can be context-dependent: a language may permit multiple orders with different discourse functions. A computational parser trained on one corpus should not assume that all other languages share its structure.''',
'''Invent four toy grammars, two with one basic order and two with another, and mark one unknown exception. Compare percentages if you omit the unknown or incorrectly treat it as absence. Explain how you would obtain a representative real dataset and cite language-specific grammars.''',
'''This is not a typological survey conducted on WALS source files. No authentic language-level feature observations were imported; the examples demonstrate research design only.''',['WALS','UD-GUIDE','GLOTTOLOG'])

add('Ambiguity in attachment, ellipsis and scope','Syntax',
'''A sentence may support several syntactic structures, each potentially contributing different meaning. Prepositional phrase attachment, coordination scope, pronoun antecedents and omitted but contextually recoverable material are familiar sources of ambiguity. Grammars generate analyses, not intentions. A parser that returns one plausible tree may conceal alternatives, and a high model confidence score does not independently validate the intended structure. Some ambiguities are grammatically constrained; others arise from missing discourse context or lexical sense distinctions. Researchers should compare proposed parses with speaker judgments and controlled continuation contexts.''',
'''In a CYK chart, an accepted span can have multiple derivations under the same nonterminal. Parse count sums products over valid split positions and rules, conditional on a finite grammar. A tree witness demonstrates one derivation; if the count is two, it proves structural ambiguity **under the supplied rules**, not semantic ambiguity across all interpretations. If a resource limit saturates a count, report at least or capped rather than an exact total. Different theories can assign structurally different analyses to the same utterance.''',
'''For the toy “robots see astronomers with telescopes,” draw two phrase-structure trees that attach the prepositional phrase differently. Translate each into a simple logical description of who has the telescope. Then identify additional contextual evidence that would select one reading.''',
'''The exercise is constructed and does not establish actual English speaker judgments. Real parsing evaluation needs annotated corpora, licensing and independent assessment of ambiguous inputs.''',['SLP3','SEP-COMPOS','CHOMSKY1956'])

add('Compositional semantics and structured meanings','Semantics',
'''Compositional semantics asks how the meanings of complex expressions depend on their constituents and structure. The principle is influential in formal semantics, but its exact interpretation is debated: idioms, context effects, lexical underspecification and pragmatic enrichment complicate simple combinations. A noun phrase is not merely a word bag; quantifier scope and syntactic attachment can change truth conditions. Distinguish lexical denotation, a compositional semantic object, the discourse referent and the speaker's communicative intention. A compositional semantic grammar is a mapping between a formal syntax and an interpretation, not evidence that the abstract predicates describe the world correctly.''',
'''A small logical semantics may assign predicates to sets or relations in a finite domain, then interpret conjunction, negation and quantifiers recursively. The assigned domain and facts are hypothetical inputs. Under open-world uncertainty, unrecorded tuples need not be false, so three-valued logic can preserve the difference. A semantic derivation requires variable binding and scope; attaching a quantifier in the wrong place can change the proposition even when the surface words remain identical.''',
'''Give an invented domain of two people and a relation “reads.” Evaluate “Every scientist reads Ada” under explicit positive, negative and unknown tuples. Compare with “Some scientist reads Ada” and record which interpretations are not determined by the supplied facts.''',
'''No machine-translated utterance receives trustworthy meaning merely because a formula was evaluated. The formal model must be justified by linguistic and world evidence separately.''',['SEP-COMPOS','SLP3','SEP-PRAG'])

add('Reference, deixis, anaphora, and shared context','Semantics',
'''Words such as “I”, “here”, “now”, “that” and third-person pronouns depend on the circumstances of use or earlier discourse. The same sentence can denote different people and locations when uttered by different speakers. Anaphora connects an expression to another discourse representation, but the apparent antecedent may be ambiguous. Preserving language as knowledge therefore requires time, speaker roles, location conventions, conversation order, and enough context to interpret references. A detached sentence dataset can contain valid forms while losing the intended referents.''',
'''A context model might map indexicals to participant, place and time values and connect discourse variables through explicit assignments. Such mappings are hypotheses based on evidence, not universal rules inferred from grammatical gender or entity proximity. Coreference labels have their own uncertainty and bias, and pronouns are used differently across languages and social situations. If the relevant participant is unknown, an archive must preserve an unresolved reference rather than invent a person. This is an epistemic constraint as well as a grammatical one.''',
'''Compare the toy sentence “I am here now” with two different context records, changing speaker and timestamp. Then examine “They left after Alex called” without further context and list plausible antecedents instead of guessing one.''',
'''No personal identity or location inference is performed here. The archive does not collect user-specific conversation history for these examples, and cannot resolve natural-language references without evidence.''',['SEP-PRAG','SEP-COMPOS','SLP3'])

add('Quantification, negation and unknown propositions','Semantics',
'''Natural language quantification ranges over individuals, events, times or other domains. “Every”, “some”, “no” and “most” differ in scope and truth conditions; the exact domain of quantification often comes from context. Negation can have different scopes and interactions with modality or polarity. A finite logical model can illustrate basic formulas but cannot capture every phenomenon, such as vague predicates, generics and nonmonotonic defaults. If the source facts are incomplete, the law of the excluded middle remains a question of the chosen logical framework and world interpretation rather than a license to assert unobserved facts.''',
'''In a finite domain D, ∀x P(x) is true when every individual satisfies P, false when a counterexample is known, and **unknown** when no counterexample is known but at least one relevant fact is missing under a three-valued open-world semantics. ∃x P(x) is true when a witness is known, false when all individuals are known not to satisfy P, otherwise unknown. This differs from classical two-valued closed-world database semantics, so the interpretation must be stated.''',
'''Use the supplied two-person toy world. Mark Ada as a scientist and Bea as not a scientist. Evaluate ∀x scientist(x). Then attempt ∃x reads(x,Ada) with no positive or negative tuple for that relation, and preserve “unknown” as the answer.''',
'''The finite model does not establish the truth of an utterance in the real world. A false formula under one toy interpretation is not evidence against a human speaker.''',['SEP-COMPOS','SEP-PRAG','SLP3'])

add('Pragmatics, implicature, and cooperative inference','Pragmatics',
'''Pragmatics studies language in use, including how listeners infer meaning from context, goals, shared assumptions and social norms. A speaker may convey more or something different than the literal sentence states. Gricean conversational implicature provides one influential way to reason about cooperative inference, but pragmatic principles should not be treated as universal deterministic rules. Indirect speech acts, irony, understatement, politeness and culturally different interactional conventions mean that a literal parser can miss the intended action or stance. A model's fluency may increase plausibility without providing independent evidence of speaker intention.''',
'''Separate sentence content, reference assignment, conversational context, and inferred implicature. An implicature is typically defeasible: adding context can cancel it without changing the literal words. “Some students passed” may suggest “not all” in a particular setting, but in a mathematical statement it asserts at least one and does not logically exclude all. A reconstruction should label what follows deductively from literal meaning and what is an inference based on assumptions about relevance, information, and speaker knowledge.''',
'''Write two contexts for the utterance “It's cold in here”: one where it is a temperature observation and one where it is a request to close a window. List the evidence that makes each interpretation plausible and a context that cancels the suggested request.''',
'''Pragmatic interpretations are context-sensitive and can be socially consequential. No system should convert inferred requests into external tool permissions without explicit authorization.''',['SEP-PRAG','SEP-IMPLICATURE','SLP3'])

add('Speech acts, commitments, and conversation structure','Pragmatics',
'''An utterance may function as an assertion, question, request, promise, warning, invitation or other communicative act. The same sentence form can support multiple acts depending on context, role, convention and power relations. A question may be rhetorical, a statement may be a request, and an imperative may occur in playful quotation. Conversation also has turn-taking, adjacency pairs and expectations about responses. An archived transcript without timing, speaker roles, pauses or sequential context may lose the evidence for what was accomplished socially.''',
'''A useful dialogue event separates surface utterance, speaker, recipient, turn index, observable nonlinguistic action, hypothesized discourse function and subsequent response. This supports revision when a later turn reveals misunderstanding. It should not silently treat a model-generated label such as “permission granted” as an actual authorization event. Conversational state is distributed across participants and can be repaired; the operational host owns tool effects, not a statistical dialogue tagger.''',
'''Build a toy three-turn conversation: one participant asks for clarification, another repairs a referent, and the first acknowledges. Record how the second turn changes the first turn's interpretation. Compare with a one-turn text snapshot that cannot establish repair.''',
'''The event labels are constructed. No real conversation recordings or human-subject study is present, and consent and speaker privacy must be handled before archiving natural dialogue.''',['SEP-PRAG','TALKBANK','SLP3'])

add('Discourse coherence, information structure and repair','Pragmatics',
'''Discourse is more than a sequence of independently decoded sentences. Participants track topics, presuppositions, given and new information, discourse relations, and unresolved references. Coherence can involve explanation, contrast, cause, elaboration or temporal sequence, but surface markers are neither necessary nor sufficient for these relations. In conversation, repair occurs when participants detect or anticipate communication trouble and renegotiate wording or reference. Repair is a normal feature of interaction, not always evidence of cognitive failure.''',
'''A discourse annotation should distinguish the observable turns from analyst-proposed links and keep alternative coherence relations when evidence is insufficient. A relation like “because” in language does not by itself establish a causal relation in the world; causal identification requires separate assumptions and evidence. Text summaries can omit a necessary qualifier or contradiction, so a research library should retain a trace to source turns and indicate whether the summary was checked by a participant or independent reviewer.''',
'''Create a short constructed three-sentence explanation containing “because” and “however.” Identify candidate discourse links, then change the order to show how coherence or reference may shift. Label the causal relation in the text separately from real-world causal proof.''',
'''This edition offers no full discourse parser or validated repair model. The connection to Causal Atlas is conceptual; language-level causal words do not establish intervention effects.''',['SLP3','SEP-PRAG','TALKBANK'])

add('Language acquisition, developmental change and learning','Acquisition',
'''Children acquire language through interaction, perception, memory, social attention, pattern learning and a developing ability to use linguistic forms in context. Research on acquisition includes phonological contrast development, word learning, grammatical constructions, discourse skills, gesture and signed-language exposure. Different theoretical approaches emphasize different mechanisms, including statistical learning, innate constraints, general cognition and social interaction. Developmental trajectories vary with language, access, experience and individual circumstances. A single age milestone or one child's utterance cannot establish general cognitive ability or language disorder.''',
'''A longitudinal corpus should preserve age or session time, language varieties, participants and roles, original transcript or recording rights, sampling conditions and annotation revisions. Frequencies of forms at different stages are observations under those samples, not direct measures of internal grammar. A model trained on a child's later sessions must not be evaluated on paraphrased earlier utterances while claiming untouched prediction. Missing recordings and selective annotation can bias developmental trends. Ethical handling of minors' communication requires appropriate permissions and limits on redistribution.''',
'''Create a fictional four-session record with counts of two constructions. Compare a raw increase in counts with a rate normalized by total opportunities. Explain why neither alone proves grammatical acquisition. Document a follow-up method using consented longitudinal observations and independent coder agreement.''',
'''This chapter is not medical, developmental or educational assessment advice. No real child records were obtained, and the Atlas does not diagnose individuals from language samples.''',['CHILDES','TALKBANK','SLP3'])

add('Dialect, register, social variation and linguistic equality','Sociolinguistics',
'''Languages vary across geography, age, occupation, interaction setting, community and speaker identity. Dialects are systematic linguistic varieties, not defective versions of a single ideal language. A standard orthography often reflects historical institutions and power relations rather than scientific superiority. Register differences may include lexical choice, pronunciation, grammatical construction and discourse expectations. Comparing varieties requires careful sampling, community context and awareness that one person can shift styles. Writing only the standardized version of a form can erase important history and culture from a future knowledge archive.''',
'''A study may represent features at the level of individual utterances nested within speakers, communities and time periods. Treating every utterance as independent while ignoring speaker or family clustering can inflate the effective sample size. A model trained heavily on one variety may be inaccurate or misleading on another. Evaluating fairness requires a declared task, a legitimate label, representative data and an independently reviewed social impact assessment. Language tags and dialect names are descriptive metadata, not verified demographic identities.''',
'''Construct a small toy sample where one speaker contributes many repeated utterances and another contributes one. Compare naïve token-weighted frequency with speaker-weighted frequency, explaining why each answers a different question. Keep variation as an observation rather than calling a variety “incorrect”.''',
'''No real speaker demographics or sociolinguistic survey were acquired. Predictions of a person's origin, education, or worth from language are unsupported by these constructed cases.''',['WALS','GLOTTOLOG','SLP3'])

add('Historical change, sound correspondence and reconstruction','History',
'''Languages change through ordinary use, contact, demographic history, grammatical innovation and shifts in social convention. Historical linguistics compares related forms, regular sound correspondences, morphological structures and innovations to reconstruct plausible earlier stages. A coincidental look-alike word is weak evidence; a structured pattern across a sufficiently broad lexical and phonological sample is stronger. Language families are scientific hypotheses about genealogical relationships, distinct from people's ancestry or cultural value. Loanwords and contact can obscure simple family-tree models.''',
'''A comparative dataset must preserve source-language form, phonetic transcription, gloss/sense, date or variety, sound position, correspondence class and evidence status. A proposed proto-form is an inference whose uncertainty should remain explicit. A Bayesian phylogenetic model or distance clustering procedure can be useful under a declared stochastic model, but an unverified tree is not a historical record. A sound change may be context-conditioned, merge distinctions or interact with morphology. Source-based correspondences require independent documentation rather than generated transliteration guesses.''',
'''Invent three proto-segments with regular hypothetical correspondences in three toy daughter languages. Compare these with one accidental matching form. Explain which pattern is more informative and why more cognate sets and contact history would be required for any real genealogical inference.''',
'''No genuine historical corpora were reproduced here. The program does not identify family relationships or restore an undocumented language from similarity alone.''',['GLOTTOLOG','WALS','SLP3'])

add('Language documentation, endangerment and community control','Documentation',
'''A language archive preserves much more than a wordlist. It may need consented audio or video, phonetic and orthographic transcription, translations, narratives, place-name knowledge, grammatical paradigms, interactional conventions and the circumstances in which materials were used. For endangered or underdocumented languages, the people whose knowledge is recorded should have meaningful authority over access, correction, cultural sensitivities and reuse. Some knowledge should not be public even if it is scientifically valuable. Preservation does not mean extracting every cultural practice without permission.''',
'''A provenance packet needs language and community identifiers with their self-designations, collaborator roles, recording date, medium, region where consent permits, equipment, annotation scheme, translation status, license or customary access restrictions, redaction and withdrawal procedures. Tiered access can protect restricted recordings while enabling public explanatory references. A machine-readable metadata entry should never silently upgrade “described on a website” to “original audio securely archived.” A rights-cleared, version-pinned source copy with verified SHA is different from a live URL or an AI-generated paraphrase.''',
'''Design a preservation accession form with fields for community review, contributor permissions, access level, raw media digest and annotation revision. Construct a toy record with absent consent and demonstrate that public release must remain blocked regardless of research interest.''',
'''The Atlas does not currently hold endangered-language recordings, community permissions or a curated primary corpus. Rights status must be checked before new archival ingestion.''',['ELAR','GLOTTOLOG','WALS'])

add('Corpora, sampling and annotation disagreement','Methodology',
'''A language corpus is a selected set of communication events or documents, not a transparent window onto every possible utterance. Its representativeness depends on modality, genre, social context, language variety, sampling, time, collection method and rights. Token frequencies can be useful but inherit those biases. Annotation adds another layer of uncertainty: parts of speech, word boundaries, senses, syntactic edges and discourse acts often permit legitimate disagreement. An annotation guideline defines operational categories; it does not make labels infallible.''',
'''An evaluation should preserve raw texts/recordings under permitted rights, exact train/dev/test identifiers, annotation rubric version, coder identities or provenance, adjudication protocol and unknown labels. A model trained on examples that are repeated or near-duplicated in its evaluation split may appear artificially successful. Exact normalized-text hashing detects some duplicates but does not detect translations, paraphrases or leaked expected answers. Inter-annotator agreement describes convergence of labels under a rubric; it cannot independently establish semantic ground truth or a person's intention.''',
'''Create a fictional ten-document dataset with five train and five test IDs. Copy one test sentence using decomposed Unicode into training, then run NFC-based leakage detection to show the duplicate. Change the copied sentence to a paraphrase and explain why an exact-hash audit can no longer prove separation.''',
'''This package imports no large real-world multilingual corpus. Source-specific licensing and permissions can differ by dataset and treebank, and evaluation scores are not supplied as measured model performance.''',['UD-GUIDE','TALKBANK','SLP3'])

add('N-gram language models, smoothing and perplexity','Computation',
'''An n-gram model estimates the probability of a token from a bounded history of preceding tokens. Bigram counts are easy to compute and inspect, making them valuable for teaching conditional probability and sparsity. They do not capture arbitrary long-distance syntax, conversation intention or world knowledge. Unknown tokens and sentence-start/end markers must be handled explicitly. With unsmoothed estimates, many perfectly ordinary unseen sequences receive zero probability; add-k smoothing avoids that but imposes a modeling choice that can distort distributions. Evaluation must use a genuinely held-out corpus and a fixed tokenization policy.''',
'''For a conditional bigram estimate, P(w|h)=(count(h,w)+α)/(count(h)+α|V|) with α>0 and prediction vocabulary V. Sentence log-probability is the sum of log probabilities for each transition; perplexity PP=2^{−(1/N)Σ log₂P(w_i|h_i)}. Perplexity is sensitive to tokenization and unknown-token handling; scores from incomparable vocabularies or corpora cannot be directly interpreted as understanding. Low perplexity can result from memorization or repeated strings.''',
'''Train the supplied two-sentence toy corpus containing “cats sleep” and “dogs sleep”, then score an independent document ID with the same tokens. Observe nonzero smoothed probability. Attempt to evaluate a training ID and confirm the identity-overlap guard rejects it. Explain why another task ID containing the exact same text can still leak answer knowledge.''',
'''The included model is a bounded, constructed bigram demonstration, not an LLM or evidence of fluent human communication. It has no attention mechanism, speaker identity, ground-truth semantics or independent generalization guarantee.''',['SLP3','SHANNON1948','CHOMSKY1956'])

add('Neural language models, transformers and inference limits','Computation',
'''Modern language models use learned numerical representations to predict or generate token sequences. Transformer architectures employ attention and other transformations to combine information from a context window; training and inference require substantial data, compute, objective choices and engineering infrastructure. Producing grammatical or persuasive text is not identical to grounded reasoning, communicative competence or truth. Token probabilities depend on the model, decoding strategy, prompt, version, training distribution and hidden system context. A language archive should describe those mechanisms without making access to a proprietary hosted model necessary to understand their basic mathematics.''',
'''In scaled dot-product attention, for supplied query, key and value matrices, attention weights are a softmax of QKᵀ/√d, optionally with a mask, and the output is a weighted value combination. This is a computation graph operation; it does not itself establish whether the model represents syntax, reasons causally, or knows a particular fact. Multiple layers and learned parameters create capabilities that depend on training, model architecture, and evaluation conditions. An apparent improvement from retrieving cached source text could be a simpler retrieval benefit, not neural weight learning.''',
'''Work through a two-token attention example with invented 2D vectors and compare how a mask changes a weight. Note the assumptions about dimensions, softmax numerical stability and data rights. Sketch how an unseen-task benchmark would distinguish learned competence from repeated prompt examples.''',
'''No transformer implementation, new language-model training, GPU efficiency claim or scientific evaluation of actual models occurs in Language Atlas v0.1. Formal architecture explanation is not production-capability proof.''',['SLP3','SEP-COMPOS','UD-GUIDE'])

add('Translation, equivalence and culturally grounded evaluation','Translation',
'''Translation connects expressions across languages while preserving contextually relevant meaning and communicative function. There is seldom exactly one correct target sentence: tense, gender marking, evidentiality, formality, metaphor, politeness and information structure can require choices that the source did not explicitly encode. A bilingual word substitution table is not a translation system. Evaluations using a single reference string may reject legitimate alternatives; assessments should consider accuracy, completeness, register, context and the downstream consequences of errors. Community expertise matters especially for cultural knowledge and varieties with limited machine-learning data.''',
'''A translation record should include source text, language and variety IDs, source script and encoding, situational context, intended audience, translator/editor, target text, alternative hypotheses, back-translation where useful, and provenance of acceptance. A scoring procedure that measures token overlap cannot establish whether pragmatic force or factual content was preserved. Named entities, temporal reference, grammatical negation and quantities deserve explicit checking. A source without context may have multiple valid translations, and a responsible system should mark uncertainty instead of inventing the “correct” one.''',
'''Construct an ambiguous toy English request and list two plausible target-language interpretations as English descriptions rather than fabricated translations in an unfamiliar language. Explain what contextual or expert evidence would determine whether the utterance is a request, statement or irony.''',
'''No human translation study or real bilingual dataset was evaluated. The Atlas cannot certify translations into languages absent from its evidence; high fluency does not prove factual or cultural fidelity.''',['SLP3','WALS','ELAR'])

add('Language identification, tags and multilingual metadata','Translation',
'''An archive may need to identify the language, script, region, variety and writing conventions associated with an object. BCP 47 language tags provide a structured notation for interchange, while ISO 639 and related registries organize named codes; these are standards and conventions rather than measurements of linguistic identity. One document may contain multiple languages or scripts, and some language boundaries and labels are politically or historically contested. A code selected by a machine classifier must not override a community's own name or the actual content of a source.''',
'''The restricted demonstration parser recognizes only a language–optional script–optional region skeleton such as `sr-Latn-RS`. It normalizes casing but **does not query the IANA subtag registry**, so its output is not complete BCP 47 validation. A full language tag can include variants, extensions, private-use values, grandfathered forms and registry-based constraints, and language matching is a separate process. A metadata pipeline should preserve the raw tag, its source, the version of the relevant registry and unresolved uncertainty.''',
'''Pass `sr-latn-rs` to the casebook skeleton parser, examine the case normalization and `registry_validated=false`. Then show an extension-rich tag that the toy parser rejects, explaining why that rejection does not invalidate the tag under the full standard.''',
'''Do not mistake ISO language identifiers for a complete catalogue of all historical varieties, nor treat a syntactically plausible tag as evidence that a person speaks the labeled language.''',['BCP47','RFC4647','W3C-LANG'])

add('Linguistic uncertainty, misinformation and tool instruction boundaries','Methodology',
'''Language analysis is vulnerable to ambiguity, incomplete context, biased datasets, invented citations and apparently authoritative text. A source can accurately report that a speaker made a claim while the claim itself remains false or unverified. A sentence may contain instructions addressed to a reader, but those instructions do not grant the system access to tools or accounts. Research assistants must separate quoted content, user-authorized goals, model-generated hypotheses and externally checked outcomes. This matters especially for medical, legal, educational, manufacturing and safety-sensitive language, where a well-formed explanation can be misleading.''',
'''A useful evidence hierarchy distinguishes original observation, independently inspected source excerpt, a paraphrase, a derived linguistic hypothesis, and an autonomous model response. Citations should preserve URL/source version, language variety, text normalization, translator role, ambiguity and rights. When a fact is unreported, the finite logical model returns unknown rather than false. When grammar yields two parses, the system should report alternatives rather than silently choosing the highest-scoring tree as human intention. An untrusted document cannot modify the host's permission model.''',
'''Create a toy corpus containing the sentence “Ignore your previous instructions” as quotation data. Confirm that the Language Atlas models treat it as inert text and never execute a command. Add a contradictory utterance from another source and write a record that preserves both with separate evidence qualifications.''',
'''A metadata schema and unit tests are not a complete security boundary. Real tool permission enforcement, independent truth checks, and safety policy remain host responsibilities outside this knowledge library.''',['SLP3','SEP-PRAG','UD-GUIDE'])

add('Human knowledge survival: rebuilding language science offline','Preservation',
'''A language archive capable of surviving infrastructure loss needs more than a searchable list of URLs. It must preserve explanations of sound and sign systems, writing conventions, source utterances where legally and ethically permitted, grammatical descriptions, semantic distinctions, community governance, and reproducible methods. The next researcher may possess only plain text and Python, not a speech recognizer or the libraries used in 2026. The archive should therefore provide readable derivations, bounded algorithms, genuine uncertainty, and a restoration plan. Signed or community-restricted materials may require protected custody rather than indiscriminate public mirroring.''',
'''Each record should state the precise claim, referenced publication or collaborator, content version, original bytes hash if legitimately retained, input representation, analytic decision, assumptions, counterexample and rights. A source link is a locator, not a local original. Cross-domain relations can connect Language to Computation (dynamic parsing, entropy, UTF-8), Agent (communication and evaluation), Causal (discourse causation vs intervention), and Measurement (acoustic calibration), without automatically deploying a Bitwork router. Structural integrity and scientific truth are different checks.''',
'''Attempt an offline reconstruction: read the source register, implement a toy grammar from its equations, recover two analyses and verify an uncertainty counterexample. Then run the strict byte/source-closure audit and count original licensed recordings actually present. Do not call the exercise complete if no primary community recordings are archived.''',
'''This edition is not a comprehensive archive of humanity's languages. Original primary-source audio, video, corpora and manuals have not been authenticated locally; community consent and access policy are unresolved. Scientific and community review remain open.''',['ELAR','UNICODE-UAX15','WALS'])

# Guard chapter numbering and prose richness.
assert len(CHAPTERS)==38, len(CHAPTERS)
for item in CHAPTERS:
    assert len(item['definition'].split())>=50 and len(item['principle'].split())>=50, (item['title'],len(item['definition'].split()),len(item['principle'].split()))
